<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ankober</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ankober is a town in the highlands of North Shewa in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, on the edge of the escarpment above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> lowlands. From the 18th century it was the capital of the rulers of Shewa, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> in the 19th century it received British and French diplomatic missions. Its importance faded after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> moved his court south, eventually founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ankober, formerly written Ankobar, lies in the North Shewa Zone of the Amhara Region at about 2,465 metres, on the eastern escarpment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a>, 40 kilometres east of Debre Birhan and about 140 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>. Its name is said to come from Anko, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> queen who ruled the town. In the mid-19th century the stone palace crowned the hill within a fence of stakes and branches, while most people lived in conical thatched huts on the mountainside, and a Saturday market served the local Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Meridazmach Amha Iyasus moved the capital of Shewa from Doqaqit to Ankober. The first Europeans to record a visit were the missionaries Carl Wilhelm Isenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf">Johann Ludwig Krapf</a> in 1839, who found a small colony of Greek craftsmen and traders; Captain William Cornwallis Harris was among the travellers who followed. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> in 1847 the Abichu Oromo attacked the town, and in 1856 the Shewans burned it themselves when Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> invaded. Rebuilt, it had about 5,000 people, rising to 15,000 when the court was in residence.</p><p>From about 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> used Ankober as a prison for political opponents, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaki-sherocho">Gaki Sherocho</a>, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>, and Ras Mengesha Yohannes, son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a>. While Menelik was away at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a>, the rebel Talha Jafar pillaged the town. On 18 October 1916, before the Battle of Segale, the troops of Negus Mikael crushed a force of 11,000 men stationed at Ankober and killed their leader, Ras Lul Seged. During the Italian occupation part of the town was moved to a more accessible plateau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ankober had a population of 2,288 in 2005 and is only the second-largest town of its woreda. Surviving buildings of the Shewan period include the church of Kidus Mikael, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a>, and a single long wall of Menelik's palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ankober” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors, at first with the title meridazmach, gradually extended their rule over the surrounding Oromo and Amhara districts, with capitals at <b>Ankober</b> and later Angolalla and Debre Berhan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li></ul></section>
